# Client-side routing: every path that is not a real file serves the app.
#
# Pages Functions run BEFORE this, so /mcp still reaches functions/mcp.js and is
# proxied to the operator rather than being swallowed by the fallback. That
# precedence is worth re-checking after any deploy that touches routing, because
# the failure is silent: the page would load and every tool call would get HTML.
#
# The target must be `/index.html` and nothing else. Pages special-cases exactly
# that path as the single-page-app fallback — applied only when no asset
# matches. Point it at any OTHER .html and the rule stops being a fallback and
# starts being a rule: measured against `wrangler pages dev`, `/* /app.html 200`
# answered `/`, `/about`, `/robots.txt` and `/llms.txt` alike with a 308 to
# `/app`, and `/* /app 200` served every one of them the same shell. Both would
# have handed a crawler HTML where it asked for robots.txt, which is the exact
# bug the current robots.txt was written to fix.
#
# So `index.html` carries the Why page's prose, prerendered, and is also what an
# unknown path answers with. A fetcher asking about `/ledger` gets the front
# page rather than the ledger — ordinary SPA behaviour, and more use than the
# empty div it used to get. The routes that are real are listed in sitemap.xml
# and named in llms.txt.
/*  /index.html  200
